[35mserver/seeds/wave3-beliefs-14-15.ts[m[36m:[m[32m671[m[36m:[m    content: "Several common misunderstandings about baptism require careful, honest correction:\n\n**Misunderstanding 1: Baptism saves you.** Some Christian traditions have taught that the act of baptism, in and of itself, washes away sin and secures salvation — a view sometimes called baptismal regeneration. 1 Peter 3:21 explicitly guards against this reading: the water does not purify the flesh; baptism is \"the answer of a good conscience toward God\" — a faith response to grace already received. Adventists affirm that salvation is by [1;31mgrace through faith[m (Ephesians 2:8-9) and that baptism is the obedient, public expression of that faith, not its source.\n\n**Misunderstanding 2: Baptism is unnecessary.** The opposite error dismisses baptism as a mere tradition with no spiritual significance. This contradicts Jesus' direct command (Matthew 28:19), His own example (Matthew 3:13-17), and the consistent practice of the apostolic church. Baptism is not optional for those who understand its meaning and have the opportunity to receive it. Deliberate refusal raises questions about whether genuine faith is operative.\n\n**Misunderstanding 3: Infant baptism serves the same purpose.** Many Christian families have their children baptized in infancy as an act of dedication or covenant inclusion. Adventists respect the sincerity behind this practice but note that the New Testament consistently presents baptism as a response to personal faith and repentance — capacities that infants do not yet possess. Dedication of children is a beautiful practice, but it is distinct from the baptism of believers that Scripture describes.\n\n**Misunderstanding 4: Rebaptism is never appropriate.** While Ephesians 4:5 speaks of \"one baptism,\" the case of John's disciples in Acts 19:1-5 demonstrates that rebaptism can be warranted when the original baptism was received without adequate understanding or genuine conversion. Adventists do not treat rebaptism casually, but they recognize that a person who has undergone a transformative encounter with God may rightly desire to be baptized in a way that reflects their current faith.\n\n**The thief on the cross.** Some point to the thief on the cross (Luke 23:42-43) as evidence that baptism is unnecessary. Adventists observe that the thief had no opportunity for baptism and that God's grace is not limited by circumstances beyond a person's control. What the thief's case does not establish is that baptism can be casually set aside by those who have both the knowledge and the opportunity.\n\n**The Great Controversy perspective.** In the cosmic conflict, baptism is a public declaration of allegiance. The candidate formally transfers from one kingdom to another. This is why the enemy often intensifies resistance around the time of baptism — the act has spiritual significance that reaches beyond the visible ceremony.",
