[35mserver/seeds/wave4-beliefs-21-22.ts[m[36m:[m[32m537[m[36m:[m    content: "Seventh-day Adventists understand Christian behavior within the framework of sanctification — the ongoing work of the Holy Spirit in the believer's life after justification. Several key convictions shape this understanding:\n\n**Grace precedes obedience.** The sequence of Romans — eleven chapters of gospel before one word of moral instruction — reflects Adventist theology at its best. Behavior change that is not rooted in an experience of God's mercy degenerates into moralism. Adventists affirm that we are saved by [1;31mgrace through faith[m (Ephesians 2:8-9) and that good works are the result, not the cause, of salvation (Ephesians 2:10).\n\n**The whole person is involved.** Paul's call to present \"your bodies\" makes sanctification tangible. It is not a disembodied spirituality. How believers eat, exercise, rest, and care for their physical health is part of the life of faith. Adventist health principles — including dietary care, temperance, and physical activity — flow from this conviction, not from cultural preference.\n\n**Transformation, not mere compliance.** The renewed mind of Romans 12:2 is the engine of genuine Christian behavior. External conformity to a set of rules without internal transformation produces the kind of religion Jesus condemned in the Pharisees. Adventist teaching at its best emphasizes heart change empowered by the Spirit, not behavioral modification enforced by community pressure.\n\n**Christ as the pattern.** John's standard — walking as Jesus walked — prevents Christian behavior from becoming an arbitrary list of dos and don'ts. Every behavioral question can be tested against the life of Jesus: Does this reflect His compassion? His integrity? His priorities? His relationship with the Father?\n\n**Corrective boundary:** Christian behavior is never a ladder to God's approval. It is t